# Working style (established over prior sessions)

- Verify empirically before asserting, especially for dbt-fabric adapter
  quirks and Power BI DAX filter-context behavior — several "should work"
  assumptions turned out wrong this way (see architecture.md's DAX
  filter-context section for the general lesson).
- When writing multi-line SQL/DAX/config via heredoc, verify the file
  landed correctly (`grep`/`cat`) before running — heredocs have silently
  truncated content mid-session before, and the resulting errors are
  confusing if you don't check the source first.
- One change at a time, verify, then the next — not several stacked
  speculative fixes at once.
- Keep methodology honest — this project deliberately favors withholding
  a number over showing a falsely-confident one (see `architecture.md`'s
  confidence-tier and "isolated contribution" framing). Don't reintroduce
  point-forecast language ("predict," "will be") when reporting on
  `Report1` measures — the dashboard was deliberately rebuilt away from
  that framing.
- Copyright/citation rules from chat also apply here when summarizing
  external research (e.g. the rockets-and-feathers literature in the
  roadmap section) — paraphrase, don't quote at length.

## Tooling split — this isn't the only place work happens

- **Claude Code (here):** dbt models, macros, seeds, SQL, git, tests —
  anything that's a file + terminal command.
- **claude.ai chat (separate):** Power BI Desktop (Windows VM GUI — DAX
  can be drafted here as text, but applying/screenshotting it happens
  there), Azure Portal / Fabric web UI clicks (IAM roles, Logic App
  designer, tenant settings), methodology discussions, LinkedIn post
  drafting. Don't assume you can drive those directly.
